---
title: Run a headless application
description: Run a service or finite network tool without creating desktop UI.
---

<span hidden data-pagefind-filter="SDK:preview.3 candidate"></span>

**Unreleased preview.3 candidate.** Published preview.1/2 SDKs do not include this
component. Use a prepared candidate SDK that passes `lui doctor`; no new public
download is implied here. [Networking examples](../networking/) use the same
runtime and public Luau services as desktop applications.

A headless entry does not create UI Instances. It can run scheduled tasks, make
requests, listen for clients and finish when its work is done. The host pumps the
existing owner scheduler, waits for native wake notifications and drains resource
retirement. Application code does not implement that loop.

## Run the complete echo example

Copy `Examples/websocket-echo` from the candidate SDK to a sibling directory.
From that project, invoke the candidate CLI with:

```powershell
lui doctor
lui check
lui run --mode headless --grant network.client,network.server --http-origin http://127.0.0.1:18083 --diagnostics development
```

The manifest declares client/server capabilities and loopback port18083. The
command separately grants them and limits outbound HTTP/WebSocket authority to
that exact origin. Port18083 must be free. A matching message is received before
the app prints success, closes its listener and exits with zero native resources.

`--http-origin` takes an exact http/https scheme, ASCII host and effective port;
it does not accept a path, credentials or wildcard. ws/wss uses its http/https
equivalent for this policy. For a client intended to access public endpoints,
`--http-public-only true` instead rejects private, loopback and other nonpublic
resolved addresses. It is unsuitable for the local echo example. Manifest address
and port restrictions remain additional restrictions. Configuring a policy never
grants a capability by itself. The direct native host supports multiple exact
origins; the current CLI option accepts one.

## Defaults and stopping

The default timeout is30 seconds, maximum3,600,000ms. A finite tool exits once
scheduled work, pending requests and listeners finish. An open listener keeps it
alive. Close a listener deliberately when a finite journey completes.

Use `--timeout-ms 60000` to allow a minute. `--poll-ms` controls the maximum wait
between owner checks, default16ms, range1–1,000ms; it does not add another
scheduler. Native completion notifications can wake the host sooner. Ctrl+C
requests interruption where the platform supports it.

| Exit | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | Work completed and native resources retired |
| 1 | Startup, app execution or cleanup failed |
| 124 | Timeout reached |
| 130 | Interrupted |

Diagnostics default to Restricted. Development mode permits explicit app prints;
those prints are application disclosure. The host's errors use bounded fixed
codes, and the CLI translates only known codes into advice. Raw stderr, private
source, paths and exception details are not forwarded as startup explanations.
Windows argument handling preserves Unicode project paths and creates no extra
console or error window.

## Common failures

- **CapabilityDenied:** pass `--grant` for each declared capability. Service names
  such as HttpService are not capability tokens.
- **HttpPolicyRequired:** choose an exact outbound origin or public-only policy.
- **PresentationUnsupported:** use an entry without UI Instances, or use the
  desktop host for the app's graphical entry.
- **ProjectPreparationFailed:** check the manifest, declared files and module lock.
- **TimedOut:** close ongoing work or choose a longer timeout. A listener is work.

Use `--stdio true` for byte-preserving pipelines, `--read-file path` for an operator-selected read-only document, and `--grant Storage` for app-private persistence. Network grants remain explicit. Select SDK-provided native libraries with `--providers Crypto,Compression,SQLite`. Each application declares its required capabilities and provider images. The host checks the captured image against its compiled target digest.

`app:GetService("PlatformService").OperatingSystem` reports the OS family. `Architecture` reports the process architecture and `BackendName` identifies the selected host. These read-only strings require no capability. Use `PerformanceService:GetTime()` to measure elapsed milliseconds.

The SDK inventories the headless host separately. The CLI validates the selected host against that inventory. See the [Stdio](../libraries/stdio/), [Compression](../libraries/compression/), [SQLite](../libraries/sqlite/) and [Crypto](../libraries/crypto/) APIs and the packaged examples. `json-lines` processes UTF-8 JSON records from stdin. `file-summary`, `digest`, `codec` and `records` demonstrate the selected file, crypto, compression and database APIs.

Windows/Linux headless liveness, real networking, denial, Unicode-path launch and
CLI failure advice have source integration checks. An SDK recipient and physical
desktop input are separate qualification gates. This is not a browser runtime or
a claim of mobile support.

## For native embedders

`LuiExecution.h` exposes a versioned pending-work query; `LuiOwnerWake.h` and the
native domain support wake/retirement. These are host/framework integration APIs,
not application-side event loops. Existing extension callback tables remain
unchanged. Keep owner-thread access, bounded resources and teardown ordering when
building a custom host.

The `live-records` notebook accepts concurrent clients. Supply `--providers SQLite --database-root DIRECTORY` to grant a dedicated private directory. Use `-- add "first record"`, `-- list` or `-- check` after the host options. Snapshot applications continue to use the existing Storage grant.

Optional text providers ship with standalone examples. `regex-filter` compiles a PCRE2 byte expression once and filters streamed lines. `toml-config` reads TOML settings and applies the Config library's schema and defaults. Select `--providers Regex` or `--providers Toml` respectively. Each target package includes its dependency license.
## Persistent services and standalone applications

Build a portable headless application with `lui build lui.json --mode headless --sdk SDK_DIRECTORY --output NEW_DIRECTORY`. Run its `Start-LUI.exe` (Windows) or `Start-LUI.sh` (Linux) directly. That launcher uses native-host switches such as `--stdio` and repeated `--provider NAME`. The development CLI uses `--stdio true` and `--providers NAME,OTHER`.

| SDK example | Operator selection | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| atomic-copy | `--write-file DESTINATION` | Streams stdin into an atomic replacement. |
| folder-copy | `--write-directory DIRECTORY` | Copies between two direct children using bounded memory. |
| live-records | `--providers SQLite --database-root PRIVATE_DIRECTORY` | Commits one notebook operation at a time across cooperating processes. |
| regex-filter | `--providers Regex -- PATTERN` | Writes matching input lines. |
| toml-config | `--providers Toml` | Validates TOML input with Config and applies defaults. |
| tool-check | `--tool EXECUTABLE --tool-cwd DIRECTORY -- ARGUMENT` | Runs a trusted native tool with literal arguments and bounded output. |

The headless operator can grant individual parent environment keys to Context with `--context true --env-keys NAME`. Child environment keys use the separate `--child-env-keys NAME` switch. The child starts with an empty environment. The selected program runs with the OS user's filesystem and networking permissions. See the packaged Process documentation for platform-specific descendant behavior. Args parses application arguments. Messages encodes small isolated values and provides a bounded mailbox. The desktop `message-viewer` example validates and displays a message interactively.

Native Release builds can additionally select `LUI_STANDARD_REGEX` and `LUI_STANDARD_TOML`. Each generated application carries its selected provider and redistribution notices.

See the [Process](../libraries/process/), [Context](../libraries/context/), [Args](../libraries/args/), [Messages](../libraries/messages/), [Regex](../libraries/regex/) and [Toml](../libraries/toml/) API references for signatures and examples.

## Parallel computation and development tools

The Windows x64 and Linux x64 candidates include six standalone projects and three optional libraries for this workflow. Create a pinned command-line project with `lui new MyTests --template spec-cli --sdk <sdk>` or `lui new MyWorkers --template parallel-data --sdk <sdk>`. The other example folders can be copied together with their module lockfiles. Use the SDK's normal `check`, `stage`, `verify`, and `build` commands.

| Example | Run it | What to observe |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parallel-data` | Headless with standard I/O | Two isolated worker VMs calculate a checksum and exit. |
| `parallel-viewer` | Desktop project mode | Edit the text field while the batch count advances, then stop both workers. |
| `source-inspect` | Headless with standard I/O, the packaged `Tools/LuiTypeCheck` executable and an explicit working directory | Send a Luau document on stdin for syntax diagnostics, literal imports and compiled size. Use the Windows checker’s `.exe` suffix on Windows. |
| `tool-session` | Headless with standard I/O, a selected Git executable and repository working directory | Send one Git object query per input line and receive a reply from the same child. |
| `tool-pipeline` | The same Git selection | Two concurrent Git stages calculate a fingerprint of the tracked path list. |
| `spec-cli` | Headless with standard I/O and application arguments after `--` | List named cases with `--list`, select a literal substring with `--filter`, or use `--repeat 10` for timings. |

The Workers, Process, Language, Spec and Random directories under `Libraries` include API signatures, limits and examples. CPU workers use independent VM memory and share the host's process. A selected executable runs under the operator's OS account. Its working directory and explicit environment grant are part of the invocation.

## Runtime consolidation candidate

The private Preview.5 SDK adds a bounded ordinary-actor FIFO through
`Workers.Pool`. Reuse the pool for measured parallel workloads and handle
`WouldBlock` when the queue fills.

The same SDK supports `lui package init`, `pack`, `verify` and `restore`.
Declare aliases and version requirements in `LuiDependencies.json`, then
run `lui package restore lui.json --source Catalog`. Run the ordinary
`check`, `run` and `build` commands on `lui.resolved.json`. Keep the
authored manifest and package lock. Subsequent restores can use cached
content offline.

Preview.5 consolidation.2 adds `NativeProviders` to package descriptors.
Each package can carry provider declarations and pinned Windows x64 and
Linux x64 binaries. Name the provider in the application's `Extensions`
list, declare its required capabilities, and restore with
`--trusted-native true`. The host still requires its own provider selection
at launch. See `PackageGuide.md` in the SDK.

`Spec.Benchmark` records bounded warmup and measured batches for
synchronous Luau operations. See the Spec library guide for counts,
percentiles and how to choose a batch size.

Explicitly trusted workers are experimental in the Preview.5 headless
profile. Use both `--trusted-ffi true` and `--trusted-ffi-workers true` on
`lui run`, then select `Workers.StartTrusted` in the app. Each trusted actor
owns its FFI session and shares the process and OS privileges.

The Preview.5 release-review SDK includes five complete application
journeys. Start with `Examples/DeveloperJourneys/README.md` for a desktop
SQLite app, a shell pipeline, a loopback service, a native CodeEditor
workbench, or a reusable native package.

Add or remove a dependency with `lui package add` or `lui package remove`.
Use `lui package inspect` to review the frozen graph. After a deliberate
change, use `lui package update`. Use `lui package restore --locked true`
for repeatable builds. The SDK guide covers explicit HTTPS and Git imports,
optional modules, target profiles and native authority.
